Here's how to implement all three requirements:

## 1. Intercept Back Navigation with a Confirmation Dialog

Override `OnBackButtonPressed` in your page:

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
            return base.OnBackButtonPressed();

        // Don't await — fire-and-forget is intentional here
        ShowUnsavedChangesDialog();
        return true; // Cancel the default back navigation
    }

    private async void ShowUnsavedChangesDialog()
    {
        bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (leave)
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    }
}
```

> **Note:** `OnBackButtonPressed` only fires for the **hardware/OS back button** (Android). It does **not** fire when the user taps the Shell navigation bar's back arrow.

## 2. Intercept the Shell Nav-Bar Back Arrow

Shell doesn't expose a nav-bar back event, so replace it with a custom toolbar item and hide the default button:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.EditProductPage"
             Shell.BackButtonBehavior="{StaticResource CancelBackButton}">

    <ContentPage.Resources>
        <BackButtonBehavior x:Key="CancelBackButton"
                            TextOverride="Cancel"
                            Command="{Binding BackCommand}"
                            IconOverride="" />
    </ContentPage.Resources>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

## 3. Wire Up the ViewModel Command

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    public ICommand BackCommand { get; }

    public EditProductPage()
    {
        BackCommand = new Command(async () => await OnBackRequested());
        BindingContext = this; // or use a separate ViewModel
        InitializeComponent();
    }

    private async Task OnBackRequested()
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            return;
        }

        bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (leave)
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    }

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
            return false; // allow normal back

        _ = OnBackRequested();
        return true; // cancel default navigation
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- **`BackButtonBehavior`** replaces the nav-bar arrow with a "Cancel" label and routes taps through your command.
- **`OnBackButtonPressed`** handles the Android hardware back button separately.
- Both paths funnel into `OnBackRequested()` so the confirmation logic is in one place.
